Date: Sun, 03 Jan 93 23:19:40 GMT
From: price@price.demon.co.uk (Michael Clive Price)
Subject: What is the ERM?  (Was Re: Sink Oceania!)

A while back I wrote

> It's debatable, but I believe only hi-tech capitalism will destroy the
> state (from within).  In the EEC we can see this process start, as
> country after country is being forced to leave the ERM due to pressure
> generated by the market on currencies pegged at artificial levels. 
> When the ERM was first begun exchanges were pegged world-wide. 
> Following the relaxation of the currency and capital markets controls
> in the 1980s their power has grown and grown.  Now many countries find
> the tables turned and economic policy has to be formulated with an eye
> on the markets.  The markets have tasted blood and they still hunger!

Following which someone asked me to explain the term ERM and its
relevance to *hi-tech* capitalism.  My apologies for dropping into
jargon which is obscure to Americans.

ERM = Exchange Rate Mechanism.  This is an agreement that exists amongst
most members of the EEC (European Economic Community - now rather
ominously just called the European Community) by which each country's
central bank will intervene to maintain their exchange rates within some
preset range of a weighted basket of the other currencies.  Don't ask
me *why* they wish to maintain fixed exchange rates.  If I tried to
explain this you would think I was making it up.

For example if the British pound sterling starts to lose value on the
currency markets (say because investors expect inflation to erode its
value in the near future) then the Bank of England is committed to step
in and start using its foreign exchange reserves to buy pounds.  Thereby
decreasing the number of pounds in circulation.  And increase their
value.  That's the theory.  But in opposing the market the BoE loses
money.  And if it fails to stop the currency slide it loses a *lot* of
money, since it is left sitting on a pile of currency it's paid over-
the-odds for.  And the speculators make a lot of money (say, was this
scheme dreamt up libertarians :-) ).

Pretty stupid idea, eh?  Well, not according to the powers-that-be.  A
year or two after the pound joined the ERM the inevitable happened.  The
strain of all the various artificial exchange rates became too much. 
Currency speculators, convinced that the rates needed re-adjusting,
moved out of lira and sterling.  The BoE couldn't carry the loses any
more and sterling (and lira) were withdrawn from the ERM and floated. 
Promptly devalued (ie sunk) and the Italian and UK governments left with
a bloody nose, no credibility and empty coffers.  Rumour has it that the
Bundesbank (the German central bank) was quite happy with the outcome
since it (sensibly) prefers floating currencies and doesn't want to
surrender its power to set interest rates according to its own wishes
rather than the rate necessary to keep the D-Mark's values pegged.

Hi-Tech capitalism enters the picture via the currency traders and the
currency market.  The currency markets are mostly electronic, with
orders for currency movements sent by wire.  Privacy and security are
largely ensured via RSA.  (Note that this is not true in the US, where
patent law has stifled the adoption of RSA as a secure encryption
mechanism.)  Because the currency markets are an electronic
transnational phenomenon, no single country can shut them down.  They
are effectively free agents.  Their power is growing and no single
government can stand against them.  Even the concerted power of most the
EEC member states didn't prevail against the power of the market.

Mike Price (price@price.demon.co.uk)

