From: pmetzger@shearson.com (Perry E. Metzger)
Subject: How Much of the Extropian Filter Have We Passed? 
X-Reposting-Policy: redistribute only with permission

>From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>

>Problem is, the above two plausibilities are together dramatically
>contradicted by the data.  With probably more than a hundred billion
>stars in our galaxy, and probably a similar number of planets, none
>has yet dramatically restructured the galaxy.  And there has been
>plenty of time.  If we're going to do it we'll probably do it in the
>next ten million years (if not the next hundred thousand!), yet there
>is no reason why life elsewhere couldn't have reached our level a
>billion or more years ago.  It would have just needed a star that was
>born a little earlier, and/or asteroid impacts or accidents of
>evolution to have happened a little earlier.

I have often argued on this list, during the last time this discussion
came up, that this may be evidence that we are the "first out the
gate".

I'll speak in this argument as if I'm describing the conditions in the
whole of the universe, but in fact the argument applies only to the
"accessable" part of the universe, meaning our light cone.

Consider that SOME species must be the first to evolve to the point
that it gains nanotechnology and hits what we sometimes call the
"singularity". Consider also that, by comparison to the length of time
it takes a species to evolve, the amount of time it takes after having
hit breakout to expand across the whole of the galaxy is tiny. Now,
there is only one intelligent species on this planet, and I would
argue this is not purely accident -- the first species to evolve
intelligence within a space likely precludes by its actions the
development of other intelligences. Similarly, I would guess that the
first species to hit breakout will expand so quickly over the universe
that it will likely preclude the development of high-tech intelligence
elsewhere.  Unless two species hit breakout almost simultaneously --
with the temporal seperation being less than the distance in
light-years between their solar systems -- its unlikely that you will
end up with two high-tech intelligent species roving about. Since you
can cross the whole galaxy in a tiny fraction of the time it took us
to evolve it would seem unlikely for this to happen. There is
therefore a very real chance that at least within a very large
light-cone we are in a winner take all game and that we might just be,
by accident of history, the winner. After all, were there aliens, they
should have been here by now.

This argument is hardly new, by the way -- the Anthropic Cosmic
Principle by Tippler and Barrow has a similar one, I am given to
understand.


Perry


