Date: Mon, 4 Jan 1993 05:27:25 -0800
From: Pamela Williard and Mark Pickens <autarch@well.sf.ca.us>
Subject: Extra-terrestrial Invaders

 
It seems to me that human progress has eight stages; each stage leading to
vastly more productivity/sophistication than the preceeding one.
 
1. Hunter-gatherer  
2. Agriculture  
3. Industry/Mass Production  
4. Computer/Information Age  
5. Nanotechnology  
6. Neuralpattern uploading
7. Personality engineering  
8. Personality integration
 
By personality engineering, I'm referring to the act of consciously changing
one's attitudes, points-of-view, emotions, etc., so as to make oneself more
effective at business, military affairs, scientific investigation, games,
and so forth.
 
By personality integration, I mean the act of merging mature, independently
evolved personalities into one being.  Whereas doing this doesn't appeal to
me personally, I can see the advantage of a being which can trace its roots
back to millions of independent entities, each with its own unique set of
contributions.
 
Let's make two assumptions.  First, life (and consciousness) does not
require divine intervention; it springs up automatically when the
appropriate physical/chemical preconditions are met.  Second, the speed of
light is the absolute limit; there's no possible way to manipulate the laws
of physics to exceed this, no matter how clever we are.
 
Now the question.  Say we encounter an extra-terrestrial intelligence (on
our turf, not their's--they come to us).  Further assume that they are
hostile and intend to evict us and seize the matter and energy in our solar
system for their own purposes.  At what stage in our development do we stand
a realistic chance of resisting them?
 
Since the universe is 10-20 billion years old and life took some three
billion years to reach from entry-level to where we are now, it's reasonable
to assume that one independently evolving intelligence will be far more
advanced than the other.  A difference of only .0001, for example, would be
hundreds of thousands of years.  Obviously, a military encounter between our
culture and one of even a hundred years behind us would be no contest.  The
chances of our two species being roughly equal in development would be
astronomically unlikely.  I can see that a more advanced species might
relate to us as we relate to bacteria.
 
However, we do have logistical advantages.  They can't bring the resources
of an entire star system to us.  All they can do is engage in a sort of
high-tech ju-jitsu; they invade us with a probe, then use our own resources
to produce more invaders and so on, until we're overwhelmed.  Our task is to
identify the invaders and attack them before they become too strong.  
 
We have quantity; they have quality.  At what point do we have enough
quality to stay in the ring?
 
I think we have zero chance before stage six (uploading).  If they think six
times faster than we do, we're in trouble; if they think six million times
faster, resistance is futile.  
 
I suspect that we won't have even a reasonable chance to stay in the game
until stage seven (personality engineering).  If you've ever played chess
with someone who has fewer tools, but a greater committment to winning than
you do, you know what I mean.
 
Mark Read Pickens   autarch@well.sf.ca.us

