From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
Subject: Re:  How Much of the Extropian Filter Have We Passed?

Here is my resolution of the problem I've posed.

Let A be the minus (based ten) log probability of a random planet
reaching our level of civilization by now.  Let B be the minus log
probability of us visibily restructing our galaxy in the next billion
years.  Pick some prior probability distribution over A and B.  For
illustration I pick the following distribution in A,B space, intended
to be a product of a wide distribution over A, and a narrower
distribution over B, reflecting my belief that it is harder to imagine
large future filters than to imagine large past ones.  (If you think
this looks implausible, fill it in with your own prior beliefs.)
 
 B ^
   |
 20|
   |   
   |
   |    
   | 
 15|.
   |
   |.   .   
   |
   |.   .   .   .   .
 10|.
   |..  .   .   .   .   .   .   .   .
   |....
   |.........   .   .   .   .   .   .   .   .   .   .   .   .
   |+...............         
  5|+++++...................... .   .   .   .   .   .   .   .
   |++++++++++...........................
   |X+++++++++++++++++++.............................   .   .
   |XXXXXXXXXXX+++++++++++++++++++++++++........................
   |XXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXX++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++........
  0----+-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+-->
   0        10          20          30          40          50  A

I take our data to be that A + B > C, where C is between 10 and 20.
That is, if A + B were too low, the chance of galaxy-changing
civilizations would be too high, and we would see stuff out there that
we don't.  To update my beliefs on this data, I just eliminate all
parts of this graph where A + B < C (and renormalize the rest).  If I
take C to be 15, I get:
 
 
 B ^
   |
 20|
   |   
   |
   |    
   | 
 15|.
   |
   |    .   
   |
   |        .   .   .
 10|
   |        .   .   .   .   .   .   .
   |        
   |            .   .   .   .   .   .   .   .   .   .   .   .
   |          ......         
  5|           ................ .   .   .   .   .   .   .   .
   |            .........................
   |             +++++++.............................   .   .
   |              ++++++++++++++++++++++........................
   |               XXXXXXX++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++........
  0----+-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+-->
   0        10          20          30          40          50  A

The bottom line is that if one's distribution for A is much wider than
that for B, A is much more affected by this data than B.  The
distribution for A is now constrained to be mostly above 15, while the
distribution for B is only pushed a little higher than it started.  If
my prior expectations about our future chances were even more
optimistic, clustered more toward zero, they would be even less
affected by this data.

Thus if you thinks its easy to imagine ways in which it is very
improbable for a planet to evolve to our level of development, but
thinks its hard to imagine ways in which it is improbable for us to
reach a galaxy changing level, then the fact that we don't see any
other galaxy changing folk out there shouldn't make you much more
pessimistic than you started out.

Note that for A+B ~= 20, the nearest neighbor is so far away that
light has yet to reach us from the big bang over there.  So if one's
prior expectations are rather spread out over A+B, odds are we won't
meet our nearest neighors for many billions of years.

