From: eder@hsvaic.boeing.com (Dani Eder)
Subject: Re:  How Much of the Extropian Filter Have We Passed?

The universe is estimated to be around 15 billion years old.
The first billion years not much happened to create life,
as the galaxies hadn't formed yet.  The first generation stars
had much lower 'metal' content (anything above H and He, in the
parlance of astrophysicists).  Thus, they would have had much
less material to build terrestrial planets out of.  These would
have been too small to hold their atmosphere.  So, we consider
the last 10 billion years as the time over which intelligent
civilizations might evolve.  Let us pick a random number of
civilizations evolving in the galaxy, say one million, with
the mean evolution time of 10 billion years, and a standard
deviation of one billion years.  We find that the first civilization
is expected by statistics to arrive 250 million years before
the second one.

What this thought exercise means is that we may be the lucky
civilization that happened to be first.  If we are first, then
we can expect to find any other life-bearing planets a long,
long way from civilization.  

If we are not first, we might expect civilizations to be
popping up with regularity about every 1000 years.  One
might expect someone to have gotten to the point of heavily
rearranging the universe.  In fact, given the same statistics,
we would calculate thus:

If large galaxies are situated on average about 10 million
light years apart (this is a guess on my part), then within
500 million light years, we should find 500,000 galaxies.  by
the above statistical argument, the first civilization 
in this volume should precede the first one in our galaxy by
2.2 billion years, which is plenty of time to fill the volume
at sublight speeds.

So we may conclude that (a) we are not the first intelligence
in this part of the universe, but something prevents large
scale spread and rearrangement of things with ruthless efficiency,
(b) we are not the first intelligence, the universe HAS been
re-arranged, and we just don't recognize it, or (c) we are
first.

Dani Eder

