estimated the total energy output.  Then I remembered that for photons,
momentum = energy/c, and that gave me a total momentum output per second
from the sun.  Assuming all that momentum could be used to push the sun,
I divided by the sun's mass to get an estimated acceleration of 4e-11
cm/s/s.

To engineer a galaxy to the point that we might notice it from a
distance, I might guess that you'd have to move stars around 10,000 lyr.
(Our galaxy's radius is about 50,000 lyr.)  Using the above acceleration,
this will take on the order of 1 billion years.

So, it does appear that there would be time for visible amounts of star
moving, but my very rough estimates do produce an answer close to the
limits at which motion might be observed.  If, say, only 10% efficiency
was possible for the momentum coupling rather than 100%, the time
estimate would increase to 3 billion years, which is less likely to be
long enough to have produced an observable effect.

