Subject: Re:  How Much of the Extropian Filter Have We Passed?

M	 DIFFICULTIES OF DETECTING INTELLIGENT LIFE


Robin Hanson says:

>With probably more than a hundred billion
>stars in our galaxy, and probably a similar number of planets, none
>has yet dramatically restructured the galaxy.
[...]
>Worse, we can look out farther into the universe, observing many
>millions of galaxies (with billions more out there), and have yet to
>see anything that looks less than natural.
[...]
>Sure, advanced folks might leave
>nature preserves, or might be a little less visible than other stuff
>-- but we don't see *anything* suspicious.

I'm so surprised by these statements. HOW WOULD YOU KNOW if there
were intelligent life in other galaxies and what exactly do you
mean by "restructuring"?  I think that Robin is engaging in some
serious hand-waving to think that we could detect life in other
galaxies (even restructured ones) with our current technology.

Yes, I know, Robin's response to me would be similar to Ray's and 
Hal's: 

>You're not thinking big enough!  I was imagining a civilization
>significantly beyond a Dyson sphere stage...

So, since I'm not thinking big enough, let me bring you back down
to earth, and demonstrate to you how difficult it is to observe
intelligent life elsewhere. This is a bit of a tangent, but I
thought that the people on this list might be interested in some
of the details involved in detecting other solar systems, since 
I have some experience in this area. My experience is in detecting
solar systems around other stars, so I don't know as much about
detecting extra-terrestrials in galaxies, but I'll comment on
that at the end of this essay.

My background:
For about 6 1/2 years, I worked with other infrared (IR) astronomers,
collecting and reducing spectrometer data from observatories at Mauna Kea, in
Arizona, and from the Kuiper Airborne Observatory (KAO). We observed whatever
was bright at infrared wavelengths (infrared: 7800 angstroms to ~1mm,
my group's spectrometers covered the wavelength range ~1 micron to 15 microns).
An object which releases most of its energy in the infrared is cooler than 
our Sun, which has a surface temperature of 6000K. Protostars and warm disks
of dust thermally radiate at about ~50-300K.  Our observations  included Solar
System objects (Mercury, the Moon, asteroids, Io, etc.), protostars, novas,
supernovas, molecular clouds, main sequence stars,  red/blue giants, white
dwarfs,  carbon stars and on and on. From about '89 to '91, I worked with a 
fellow named Dana  Backman to investigate  types of stars called "infrared-
excess" stars. Our goal was  to come up with a list of 100-200 nearby 
infrared-excess stars to give to the SETI  (now  renamed the HRMS for High 
Resolution Microwave Survey) folks as their main  target  list. (I think 
that they have about 50-80 from us now.) 

Infrared Astronomy
The advantages of infrared astronomy are that it can yield information
on areas not visible to optical astronomers and on a smaller angular
scale than is resolvable by radio astronomers. 

The disadvantages are that infrared astronomy is fairly difficult to 
perform. Why? Water vapor, carbon dioxide, and ozone in the Earth's
atmosphere absorb much of the infrared radiation  from space. Only a few
"windows" of transparency exist at infrared wavelengths. Also, all lukewarm
bodies emit copiously in the infrared, so that a background "pollution" of
infrared radiation can swamp any signal coming from distant astronomical
sources.  Another observational problem occurs due to the infrared radiation 
contribution of  interstellar dust between you and the object you're trying to
observe. For example, IR emission from warm  interstellar dust has often turned
out to be more powerful than the IR emission of HII regions of  molecular clouds
where  star-forming is thought to be occuring.  The background contamination 
can be partly  eliminated by "chopping" (looking on  and off the astronomical 
source, and differencing the two signals to subtract out  the background), 
but statistical  fluctuations can limit the accuracy of the  measurement. 
Another technique used to  reduce background IR radiation is to cool the 
spectrometer down to liquid nitrogen  or liquid helium temperatures, and 
in the  case of the KAO, the telescope cavity is cooled too. 

Infrared Excess Stars
What are infrared excess stars? This is a general term used to describe
stars that have IR emission in excess of their Planck blackbody continuum. IR
excesses are usually believed to originate from dust grains. The origin of
dust grains varies among stars of different ages and spectral classes. We 
were interested in middle-aged (on the main-sequence in the Hertzsprung-
Russell diagram), single stars, most similar to  our own Sun. IR excesses 
from these types of stars are usually caused by dust grains located in a 
shell or disk around the star. Stars with these disks or shells are assumed
to have either: 1) a solar system like our own, or 2) a "failed" solar system,
i.e. conditions weren't conducive to protoplanets condensing out of the rotating
disk around the star. The infamous beta Pictoris disk-star is probably a
"failed" solar system.

One Procedure to Determine if Star is an IR Excess Star and Related Physics
-Take a star like the sun. Given its temperature and radius, and
assuming it is a blackbody, find its total luminosity, peak blackbody
emission and wavelength of maximum blackbody emission.
-Take an object the mass of the moon, and turn it into grains of
100 micron in radius and density of 3 gr/cm^3. Find the total
cross-sectional area of the grains in the swarm.
-Put the grains in orbit around the sun-like star at a distance
of 30 AU and assume that the grains radiate like blackbodies
(albedo = 0). Determine their temperature and  peak blackbody emission.
-Take the system of star + grains and observe it from 10 pc. Determine its
Vmag (visual magnitude ~5500 angstroms) from its temperature, size,
and distance. Convert it's visual magnitude to flux. Note the wavelength
that your IR detector is sensitive at, say x microns. What would
this flux be at x microns? Now compare it to the flux from a regular
star w/no disk at x microns. If the (system + grains) flux is greater than 
"y" flux (determined by a variety of empirical and theoretical means) then 
it makes it onto our list as an infrared excess star.
[Note, we determine observationally the Vmag of the hypothesized star + grains
system.]

Abundance of Planetary Systems
(From the results of Backman and others.) If the abundance of cool grains around
main sequence stars are each a sign of a planetary system, then planets are
indeed plentiful in our region of the galaxy. The first factor in the 
infamous Drake equation f_p, the fraction of stars with planetary systems,
is estimated as >= 0.15.

Observing "Us" Using Our Current Technology
If an intelligent species with our level of technology were on a nearby star,
could they detect our Solar System?

	The surprising answer is: Probably not. 

(This explanation comes from Backman.)  Say that the instrument used is one like
the Infrared Astronomical Satellite (IRAS) built by the Dutch and used to
perform  an all-sky survey in January to November 1983. The satellite 
contained a 57 cm telescope and 4 individual fields-of-view detecting IR 
sources at 12, 25, 60, and 100 microns. And assume that the nearby star is 
beta Pictoris at 16 parsecs  (1pc = 3.26 light years). Now, the reasons 
for indetactability.

Reason 1: The sun itself would be detectable at 12 and 25 microns. The sum of
infrared flux from the planets, moons, and large asteroids would be a factor
of 10^4 below IRAS' best sensitivity limit. 

Reason 2: The bolometric luminosity of the zodiacal dust in the inner solar
system is about 8x10^{-8} solar luminosities, with typical temperature 230K,
and typical grain sizes of 100 microns. The presence of that material would
produce, at the most favorable contrast, < a tiny excess of 2x10^{-4} janskys,
less than 10^{-2} of IRAS' detection limit. Also, the spatial emission would not
be resolved separately from the sun.

Note that optical or infrared radiation from extra-solar planetary material is
sensitive  to area rather than mass. So that the zodiacal dust contains less
than 10^{-10} of the total mass of the planets, yet has an infrared luminosity
100 times larger.

Reason 3: The classical Oort cloud from which long-period comets are thought to
originate contains roughly 100 earth-masses in comet nuclei with typical radii
of 10 km. Our Oort cloud would subtend a diameter of 5 degrees when viewed from
beta Pic. Even though the cloud is spatially resolvable, the comet-sized bodies
do not render a suitable detectable thermal luminosity. The total surface area,
and thus the luminosity is too small. In addition the temperature would be
<= 5K, and its emission would be difficult to pick out in contrast with the
galactic and cosmological background radiation.


Comments on Observing Exterrestrials in Other Galaxies.
Stars with planets are difficult enough to detect as described above, 
even though there may be many out there. What sort of restructuring could
be done to galaxies to make it resolvable to infrared astronomers? The
restructuring would have to be done so that a large region 
that's spatially resolvable from the galactic center is radiating brightly 
in the infrared. The surface area and the temperature are the important pieces
for the infrared astronomers. And then to detect "intelligent" life, the
restructuring has to be done in a way that makes it appear "unnatural." I guess
I'll leave this kind of speculation to the more creative folks  out there!

